| Gaussian, Kendall inversion | 0.583 | 0.425 | 0.476 | ∞ | 365.69 | -725.37 | 0.000 |
| t, MPL | 0.589 | 0.432 | 0.465 | 4.33 | 419.76 | -831.52 | 0.290 |
| t, Kendall inversion + MPL for df | 0.583 | 0.425 | 0.476 | 4.31 | 419.51 | -831.02 | 0.287 |
| Generating t copula | 0.600 | 0.400 | 0.500 | 4.00 | | | 0.314 |

| Event | Data | Gaussian fit | t fit | Generating copula |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| P(all three below their 5% quantile) | 0.0100 | 0.0050 | 0.0083 | 0.0087 |
| P(asset 2 below 5% q. given asset 1 below) | 0.420 | 0.308 | 0.392 | 0.401 |
| P(all three below their 1% quantile) | 0.0010 | 0.0003 | 0.0013 | 0.0015 |
| P(asset 2 below 1% q. given asset 1 below) | 0.400 | 0.182 | 0.331 | 0.358 |

| Truth | n | Mean rho12 (MPL) | Mean rho12 (tau) | Mean df (MPL) | Median df (MPL) | AIC picks t |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Gaussian copula | 250 | 0.606 | 0.596 | 471.2 | 103.5 | 0.100 |
| Gaussian copula | 1000 | 0.601 | 0.599 | 651.1 | 1000.0 | 0.020 |
| t copula, df = 4 | 250 | 0.601 | 0.602 | 3.9 | 3.7 | 1.000 |
| t copula, df = 4 | 1000 | 0.599 | 0.601 | 4.0 | 3.9 | 1.000 |
\image(figures/copulas.svg)
// result_end

Because the fit uses only ranks, the skewed, heavy-tailed margins of the returns do not affect it. Both families recover the correlation structure: MPL and Kendall inversion agree to within 0.02 of each other, and within sampling error of the generating 0.6, 0.4 and 0.5. The likelihood separates the families clearly. The t copula improves the log-likelihood by 53 for one extra parameter, and its \(\hat\nu=4.3\) is close to the generating 4.

The families differ most where it matters for risk. The data show all three assets below their 5% quantile on 1.0% of days. The t fit implies 0.83% and the generating copula 0.87%, but the Gaussian fit implies only 0.50%. At the 1% level the Gaussian copula understates the joint-crash probability fivefold (0.03% against 0.13–0.15%). The figure shows why. As \(q\to0\), the Gaussian conditional probability \(P(U_2\le q\mid U_1\le q)\) falls towards zero, whatever the correlation. The t copula's levels off near its tail-dependence coefficient \(\hat\lambda=0.29\) (the horizontal line), as do the data, until too few points remain to estimate it.

The simulation confirms that both correlation estimators are unbiased, and that \(\nu\) is well identified from a t₄ copula (median \(\hat\nu\) 3.7 at n = 250 and 3.9 at n = 1000). When the truth is Gaussian, \(\hat\nu\) is usually very large and often reaches the upper bound of 1000, which is the boundary of the parameter space. Its mean is therefore not meaningful. AIC picks the t copula in 10% of Gaussian samples at n = 250 and 2% at n = 1000, and always when the truth is t₄. With Gaussian data, choosing the t copula costs little, because a large \(\nu\) reproduces the Gaussian. Choosing the Gaussian copula for t data understates joint extremes.

>> Practical guidance

- Fit copulas to pseudo-observations unless the margins are known. Ranks protect the dependence estimate from misspecified margins, which is the main point of the copula approach.
- Prefer the t copula as a default for financial, insurance and environmental data, where joint extremes matter. Check the evidence for finite \(\nu\) with AIC or a likelihood-ratio test, keeping in mind that \(\nu=\infty\) lies on the boundary.
- Kendall inversion is fast and robust, and is a good start or check for MPL. In high dimension, project the inverted matrix if it is not positive definite.
- Elliptical copulas have the same dependence in both tails, and one \(\nu\) for every pair. If losses cluster more than gains, or tail dependence differs across pairs, use asymmetric (Clayton, Gumbel) or vine copulas.
- Report tail dependence and joint-exceedance probabilities alongside the correlations. Correlation alone does not describe the risk of simultaneous extremes.

>> References

- A. Sklar (1959). Fonctions de répartition à n dimensions et leurs marges. *Publications de l'Institut de Statistique de l'Université de Paris* 8, 229-231.
- C. Genest, K. Ghoudi and L.-P. Rivest (1995). A semiparametric estimation procedure of dependence parameters in multivariate families of distributions. *Biometrika* 82(3), 543-552. doi:10.1093/biomet/82.3.543
- S. Demarta and A. J. McNeil (2005). The t copula and related copulas. *International Statistical Review* 73(1), 111-129. doi:10.1111/j.1751-5823.2005.tb00254.x
- A. J. McNeil, R. Frey and P. Embrechts (2015). *Quantitative Risk Management*, revised ed., ch. 7. Princeton University Press.
- M. Hofert, I. Kojadinovic, M. Mächler and J. Yan (2018). *Elements of Copula Modeling with R*. Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-89635-9
- D. Lewandowski, D. Kurowicka and H. Joe (2009). Generating random correlation matrices based on vines and extended onion method. *Journal of Multivariate Analysis* 100(9), 1989-2001. doi:10.1016/j.jmva.2009.04.008
